The campaign's 21 commits, squashed because the code churned a lot across five sessions and the diff is the only
reviewable form.  Nothing in the delivery changes: this is the prototype tree on the r368 block set (base
919794500, block tip af7a57ba8).

What it does, in one paragraph: it replaces the slot-ring staging transport with a one-to-three-buffer PIPELINE
whose buffers live in the slab's transport region, makes that transport the default at prefill width behind a
measured selector (host bytes the gather would pull per token vs 38 us), and fixes the class of defects that only
a real server's SECOND prefill reaches (the bump cursor was never returned, the carve was never released, a
decode/verify compute-band raise was deferred for ever).  The region is sized from one split's host-weight set,
re-carved per wide pass, sits on a cushion of N compute chunks above the decode/verify band so that band can grow
without moving it, and its DEPTH is chosen from the expert-cache residency once the arena settles.  Around it: the
hybrid/bulk fill for a tensor split, the merge-floor calibration to the device link, and the arena settle
re-opening when capacity grows.

The review (wip/flash-next-prefill/REVIEW-squashed.md) and the maintainer's decisions, all in this commit:

  * two format-string bugs (a dropped argument in the transport-decision line, a %zu fed an int in the
    transport-exhaustion WARN), a Meta iface member default-initialised instead of declared;
  * dead code removed: the free-VRAM start-up sizing, the ring-era per-split width sizing, a stale Meta slot
    default, duplicated comment blocks;
  * J1: the buffer-count override is clamped to the pipeline's own 1..3;
  * J2: the per-graph split list is 512 (measured ceilings: Flash-Next 144, 122B-A10B 120) and warns once when it
    fills, because past it those splits take the serial upload -- the path this transport exists to replace;
  * J3: the residency mirrors are O(1) (measured the old scan first: 0.3 us/call, ~150-200 us per run against
    ~170-990 ms drop pauses).  The scan doubled as a safety net, so the one path that cleared `t.slots` without
    the accounting now calls it, and the exit census re-derives both mirrors and warns on drift;
  * J4: the transport's buffers are the PIPELINE's -- `GGML_SCHED_PIPELINE_BUFS` / `GGML_SCHED_PIPELINE`,
    `sched_pipe_*`/`pipe_*` throughout, and the environment is parsed in ONE place (the CUDA authority) with the
    scheduler and Meta reading it through `slab_ring_slots`.  `GGML_SCHED_STAGE_SLOTS` is kept as the one alias
    (the released block documents it); the campaign-internal `GGML_SCHED_PINGPONG_BUFS` is dropped, not aliased;
  * `GGML_CUDA_SLAB_TRANSPORT_MIB` is RETIRED ENTIRELY: the region is one buffer x the depth, so the knob named no
    resizable quantity and could only cap the region below what a buffer needs.  No tombstone in the code.

Two field defects found during the end-user test, both fixed here:

  * A fill was issued BEFORE the slab had carved the transport region (the carve is released at every
    prefill -> decode transition and re-made when the wide compute view is placed), so the first staged ubatch of a
    prefill was refused and fell back to the SERIAL whole-table upload -- 40 GB through the pageable path.  On a
    single-ubatch prompt that is the whole prefill: measured 12.32 s / 47 t/s on the field shape.  The selector
    now asks whether a region is carved and GATHERS when it is not (the next ubatch stages): the same 239-token
    prompt reads 338 t/s, a 581-token one 255 t/s, and the 89k arm is unchanged (43d0b1f4629e, 2457 t/s).
  * The pipeline buffers were sized from the FIRST split set filled, so a graph whose sets differ (231 MiB against
    450 MiB on the field shape) reallocated mid-graph and warned "this should not recur" on every short prompt.
    They are now sized once, from the graph's largest set.

The pre-promotion review (no tombstones for dead code) then removed the two D1 probes -- their measured negatives
live in the campaign's record, not in a default-off kernel and an env var -- and fixed four defects it found:

  * the prefill -> decode release, and the fill's wait on the buffer's free event, both assumed ONE backend.  A
    `-sm layer` split keeps its stage slots per backend and need not fill a buffer with the split that consumes it,
    so the release now runs on every backend's slots 0..n-1 and the wait names the buffer's PREVIOUS owner;
  * `pipe_n_ent` was not cleared per graph, so a graph that staged nothing could match a STALE entry against this
    graph's input copy (the copies are allocated from one reused context buffer, so the same address comes back)
    and consume an unfilled buffer.  Every entry is dropped at the top of each graph;
  * `h2d_stage_release` called `arena_free` for a transport slot.  The slot lives inside the work band, where
    `arena_free` either no-ops (below the boundary) or would hand transport-region VA to the arena (above it); the
    region's bump cursor, which the release already resets, is the reclaim;
  * the `transport buffer:` line printed its two provisional numbers in the wrong order (and built a char* from a
    temporary std::string in a ternary).

Verified: 1 GPU 89k greedy text 43d0b1f4629e on every run; the field three-request sequence reproduces to the MiB
(decision 3 -> 2, region 3412.5 -> 2275.0 MiB, carves 2304, arena after each drop 15 960.2 / 15 960.2 / 15 827.7
MiB); short-prompt prefills stage the gather arm and never take the serial fallback; the exit census reaches its
summary with no mirror drift.

Post-review rework (the maintainer's calls, same day):

  * `slab_ring_*` -> `slab_stage_*` throughout (`ggml_backend_dev_slab_stage_region` / `_set_stage_cap` /
    `_set_stage_unit` / `_stage_slots` / `_stage_set`, `ggml_cuda_slab_stage_*`, `h2d_stage_cap_bytes`, the
    `stage_region`/`stage_used`/`stage_slots`/`stage_requested` members), so the internal names match the
    vocabulary every log line and every doc entry already uses; comments that named the renamed symbols follow.
  * the buffer-count table is re-cut and gains a 0: one buffer would take the arena below the slab's minimum
    (`GGML_CUDA_SLAB_MIN_ARENA_MIB`, 2048 MiB) -> **0 buffers = GATHER** (no carve, so the selector gathers);
    two buffers the same -> **1**; three buffers leaving < 40 % of the host set -> **2**; else **3**.  All four
    branches measured: 122B 3 GiB-capped arena -> 0 (933 t/s), 4 GiB-capped -> 1 (1029), auto -> 2 (1199),
    35B-A3B Q8_0 1 GPU -> 3 (3214), Flash-Next IQ4_NL 2-dev tensor -> 3 (2607).
  * **the UNIT was 2.5-3x too big** (the latent defect behind the wide boundary sitting ~2 GiB higher than it
    needs to): `llama_context` published the per-LAYER sum of a layer's host expert tables, but a scheduler split
    stages ONE routed role, so the region over-carved by 1.3-2.1 GiB on every measured shape -- 122B 1686 vs 630
    MiB per buffer, 35B-A3B 816 vs 272, Flash-Next GSQ 1137 vs 450.  The unit is now the largest host expert
    TENSOR per device (one split input), `max_host_expert_layer_bytes()` is deleted with it, and the freed region
    is arena the wide pass does not have to evict.  Text unchanged (`43d0b1f4629e` x3 on the 89k arm), prefill
    neutral on the 89k/122B arms, +3.5 % on the 35B (3213.7 vs 3105.5), and the staging census is the same work
    with 2.5-3x less region.
  * `GGML_CUDA_GRAPH_MAX_TOKENS` is CLAMPED to 1..32 (it was unbounded) and `SCHED_STAGE_CAPTURE_SAFE_TOKENS`
    moved to the same 32, so a captured graph can never contain an in-place staged consume: captured => <= 32
    rows, in-place consume => > 32 tokens.  A value above the cap warns and is clamped.
  * recorded, not changed: the `8832 MiB` wide boundary is correct by design (band 1536 + cushion 512 + region
    3456 at depth 3 + wide view 3328); and the field shape's reserve partition varies between runs of the SAME
    commit with the heat prior (3211+1536+1536 vs 3634+768+768), so an A/B on it must pin `MOE_EXPERT_HEATMAP`.

Hand-over finding (the maintainer asked whether staging still works on a `-sm tensor` split):

  * ONE GPU: yes -- `-sm tensor` over a single device is a plain CUDA backend, so the PIPELINE stages (that is the
    `144 splits x 2 x 450 MiB` in every field log).
  * TWO OR MORE: no, and three things had to be fixed before that was even visible.  (1) this campaign's review
    rewrote the copy-input gate to `pipe_on && entry != -1`, which is FALSE on a Meta split -- the base gated on
    `stage_split_ok` -- so the Meta backend's staged branch became unreachable; the split now carries its own
    `stage_ok` (computed through the same gather/width gates).  (2) the meta DEVICE forwarded none of the
    transport-region hooks (`slab_stage_set`/`_region`/`_slots`/`_set_stage_cap`/`_set_stage_unit` were nullptr,
    unlike its narrow2/compute_plain forwarding), so its per-device slabs were never armed; they now forward.
    (3) the per-device region is STILL never carved on a split -- the wide buffer is allocated once at reserve and
    reused, and the `llama_context` drop that would re-reserve it is gated on a region already existing -- so every
    ring slot is refused and `GGML_SCHED_DEVGATHER=0` takes the serial whole-split upload (2-dev GSQ 89k: gather
    3431-3462 t/s vs DEVGATHER=0 3284-3299).  The default gathers (the measured-better arm) and no validated shape
    changes (89k 2488.4 t/s, 43d0b1f4629e; field 144 x 2 x 450 MiB, no refusal/drift/grow).

  OPEN for the promotion decision (`REVIEW-squashed.md` §9): delete the Meta staged transport (unreachable in
  practice, behind the gather on every arm measured) or finish it (carve on the arm, un-gate the drop).  The docs
  must not claim tensor-split staging until one of the two is done.

Session 7 -- the FINAL review pass on this tree (read as a reviewer, not as its author), then the tests, then STOP
for the block decision.  Seven defects, all fixed here; the campaign's numbers are unchanged by them (re-measured
after the fixes, see `wip/flash-next-prefill/RESULTS-session7.md`):

  1. `ggml_backend_i.moe_cache_residency` was inserted in the MIDDLE of a struct whose members are the positional
     ABI contract ("APPENDED so the other backends' positional initializers stay valid").  `ggml_backend_cpu_i` and
     `ggml_backend_rpc_interface` do not list it, so every row after it -- `cpy_tensor_async_nowait` through
     `moe_cache_prefill_stream` -- shifted by one in both.  Every shifted row happens to be NULL today, which is
     exactly why it would have survived until someone filled one.  MOVED to the end.
  2. The rework dropped BOTH released staging gates from the scheduler and left the hooks they were made of
     dangling: `moe_cache_in_place` (TODO #133 -- an identity/fully-resident table is served from the ARENA, so the
     pipeline's fill would be a whole-table re-copy out of VRAM that nothing reads: 61 GiB per 3049-token prompt on
     a 100 %-resident arena) and `moe_cache_stage_ready` (the D2D-era trade).  RESTORED the in-place guard in
     `sched_pipe_split_set` (all-or-nothing per split, like the gather); did NOT re-wire `stage_ready`, because a
     pipeline fill lands IN PLACE -- the trade it encoded is gone, and honouring it here would refuse staging for a
     whole first prefill (`!g_sized`).  Its campaign-added `MOE_HOST_POOL_STAGE_STANDDOWN` branch went with it: it
     was a dead knob whose documented effect no longer existed.  Whether the now-callerless hook is retired is part
     of the block decision, not of this change.
  3. The new "release the CARVE at the narrow placement" block was a latent path back to the serial upload: it
     zeroed `stage_region`, which is the value `llama_context` gates its narrow -> wide layout drop on
     (`ring_carved`).  A decode width that places a narrow view AFTER the scheduler released its buffers would drop
     the carve, the next prefill would reuse the narrow compute buffer, no wide view would be placed, the region
     would never be re-carved -- and the split would then either gather for the whole prefill or take the serial
     whole-table upload (the 12.3 s / 47 t/s field defect).  It also returned no VRAM: the region lives inside the
     work band, so while the wide pass is disarmed the boundary has already shrunk below it and the arena owns that
     VA (`arena_alloc_transient` additionally requires `wide_armed`; the field log's `context-exit` line reads
     `work=2048 transport=960 arena_region=18368`).  REMOVED; a depth change still drops an idle carve in
     `ggml_cuda_slab_set_stage_region`, which is the one job it did correctly.  The BUFFERS are still released at
     every prefill -> decode transition (`h2d_stage_release` -> `transport_reset`), unchanged.
  4. `llama_context`'s new `if (unit == 0) return;` is a `return` from a CONSTRUCTOR body: a model with a
     host-expert map but no per-device slice would have abandoned the rest of the constructor (draft vocabulary,
     scheduler reserve, output buffers) and left a half-built context.  It skips the transport sizing instead.
  5. `GGML_SCHED_STAGE_MIN_TOKENS=0` ("stage for every batch") could never reach the selector: `pipe_wide_cur` (and
     therefore `pipe_wide_prev`, which is what lets a wide graph stage at all) was only marked when the gate was
     > 0.  A gate of 0 now counts as wide above `SCHED_STAGE_CAPTURE_SAFE_TOKENS`, which is the same in-place
     invariant the cap exists for.
  6. The zero-carve test read `GGML_SCHED_DEVGATHER` as `== 1`, so the FORCE arm (2) carved a region it never
     stages -- an A/B measuring the arena with the thing it removes.  It is `!= 0` (both gather modes gather a
     pool-backed table).
  7. The hybrid fill counted its arena-side census bytes BEFORE the host DMAs, so a failed hybrid fill reported
     them twice once the two-tier fallback accounted the same fill.  Census only, but the census is the evidence.

Re-measured on the fixed tree (all in `RESULTS-session7.md`): 89k 1-GPU `2492.4 / 2478.8` (median 2485.6),
`43d0b1f4629e` on both cycles; the census arm stages `138 splits x 3 buffers of 450 MiB` with 0 refusals; the field
sequence reproduces session 6 to the pattern (`3 -> 2`, `144 x 2 x 450 MiB`, band 1536 -> 1792 -> 2048 all absorbed
by the cushion, `overrun_deferred=0`, no refusal/drift/grow); the 2-device `-sm tensor` default gathers (3429.3 t/s,
the pipeline's one decline line, no Meta-ring attempt); and ALL FOUR buffer branches are reached -- 3 (35B-A3B Q8_0,
3210.4), 2 (122B auto 1196.8 / cap 4096 1201.6), 1 (cap 3072, 1028.4), 0 (cap 2560, 930.8, `GATHER`, no refusal).

STOP: promotion is a block decision (`AGENTS.md`) -- block 09 for the selector + the re-keyed zero-carve rule,
block 10 for the pipeline / transport region / cushion / depth authority and the VMM pool's fail-soft growth -- and
the §9 Meta transport question (delete it or finish it) is a design choice.  Nothing is cut, renumbered or folded
without the maintainer's explicit answer.

Session 7 follow-up -- the maintainer's instruction after the review ("retire moe_cache_stage_ready() and leave zero
tombstones"), plus the two interface warnings the retirement's neighbourhood exposed:

  * **`moe_cache_stage_ready` is RETIRED**: the `ggml_backend_i` hook, `ggml_backend_cuda_moe_cache_stage_ready`
    (wrapper + `.cuh` declaration + iface row), `ggml_backend_meta_moe_cache_stage_ready` (forwarder + row), the
    `ggml_backend_cpu_i` / `ggml_backend_rpc_interface` rows, and `moe_cache_stage_ready()` in
    `moe-expert-cache.{h,cu}`.  It has had no scheduler caller since the pipeline replaced `sched_stage_issue`, and
    the D2D-era trade it encoded does not exist for an in-place fill.  No tombstone: the removal leaves no comment
    explaining what it was, and the one comment elsewhere that named the "two transport queries" now names the one
    that remains.
  * **the positional initializers are COMPLETE again**: `ggml_backend_cpu_i` / `ggml_backend_rpc_interface` now list
    the trailing `stage_release` and `moe_cache_residency` rows (the previous commit left them implicit, which is
    legal but is exactly the shift hazard the append rule exists for), and both device interfaces now list the
    trailing `.gtt_store_arena_min` row they had been missing since that hook was added.  The build is free of
    `-Wmissing-field-initializers`; the only warning left anywhere is upstream `tests/test-backend-ops.cpp`'s
    `-Wreorder-ctor`, in a file neither the delivery nor this campaign touches.
  * verified on the final tree: 89k 1-GPU `2427.9` (the box's low phase) then `2494.1` t/s, `43d0b1f4629e` on both
    -- a dead hook's removal and nullptr initializer rows cannot move behaviour, and the hash says they did not.

Session 7 follow-up 2 -- the Meta (tensor-split) staging transport is WIRED IN and MEASURED, on the maintainer's
instruction ("wire it in, measure, then decide the policy; keep the support whatever the policy is"):

  * `sched_stage_exposure_decides` no longer refuses staging for a backend without backend slots.  That guard encoded
    a STRUCTURAL conclusion ("the pipeline needs slots, therefore the gather owns this tier") that had never been
    tested against the transport such a backend actually has -- and it made the transport unselectable by any policy.
    It now accepts EITHER staging form -- backend slots (the pipeline, in-place) or the backend's own `stage_input`
    (per-device slots, filled on each device's copy stream and copied into the split input; the redirect is not
    correct for an offloaded expert table, so no in-place there) -- and leaves the transport to the exposure test.
    Both forms take their depth and region from the same authority (`slab_stage_slots` ->
    `ggml_backend_cuda_transport_bufs`, `unit x depth`, capped), so the ring cannot drift from the pipeline.
  * MEASURED, 2 cards `-sm tensor`, GSQ-IQ3_XXS 89k, `-ub 8192`, the maintainer's reproducible flags
    (`-lme mirror --lazy-mode off`; per-arm spread 0.1-0.5 %, against up to 25 % on the pool tier + lazy on):
        40 % coverage: staged 2869.8 vs gather 2655.3  -> staged +8.1 %   (n=3 each)
        60 % coverage: staged 3009.6 vs gather 2910.9  -> staged +3.4 %   (n=2 each)
        80 % coverage: staged 3100.0 vs gather 3164.7  -> gather +2.1 %   (n=2 each)
        ~100 %        : staged 3428.2 vs gather 3429.8 -> tie
    Crossover ~70 % coverage, one arm from the exposure rule's ~78 % prediction for this set -- the tensor split obeys
    the same physics, and the staged transport is worth 8 % at the low-coverage end.  The DEFAULT arm follows the
    rule on this tier (STAGE at 40/60 %, gather at 80/100 %).
  * The tensor split does NOT get the pipeline's fill-ahead or in-place consume yet, and that is structural, not a
    shortcut: the Meta drain consumes every pending entry per `graph_compute` (a prefetch would be drained early and
    serialize -- it needs a per-split drain plus a `stage_prefilled` split flag), and the in-place redirect is
    measurably incorrect for an offloaded expert table.  Recorded as the next step, with the win it is worth.
  * RETENTION: the Meta transport stays reachable and selectable in every future policy (the selector accepts both
    forms; `GGML_SCHED_DEVGATHER=0|2` are the two controls).  A future structural refusal is a maintainer decision.
